-- world.lth: the world as a token threaded through actions, what an action may change, externs
-- as routes validated at their first action, and the parts of the world a program touches.
--
-- Draft 0, 2026-10-13, by Claude Fable 5.1 in the session (step 6.1 of
-- record/plan/luthier-from-zero/plan-from-zero-2026-10-13-execution.md, brought before evidence by
-- D-2026-10-13-ad: execution first, so that sonolocalización runs uncertified and the
-- certificates are designed on how it fails). Sources: spec 10.S1 to 10.S13 (an action, the token,
-- the core's table, two kinds of action, externs as routes, the bridge, named worlds); L11; L4.5;
-- D4 and D8 of record/plan/archive/plan-implementation-2026-10-13-decisions.md (the token used
-- exactly once; `W` optional in `main`); D-2026-10-13-a (one world), -b (`W` a keyword), -g.
-- Pattern of ebnf.lth: a theory, its laws over a model, `T := laws`.
--
-- What this file states. The core knows of the world only the table of 10.S5: the token `W`, a
-- keyword with no `==`, which a program never constructs; `act`, applying an action to a token;
-- `own`, whether an action concerns Luthier's own store. Everything else is a library's (10.S5,
-- D-2026-10-12-al): what the parts of the world are, what an observation of one is, how a class
-- of machines is described. So the theories here are stated over `W` itself, and what the library
-- adds, the representation of `W` and the observations of it, stays private by ascription (L6.12):
-- a model of `World` says what is read at each place. The core asks: an action is a function
-- over the token with its result (10.S3); it changes only what its declaration says (10.S7); an
-- action on Luthier's own store is idempotent and a world action never is (10.S6); an extern is a
-- route, read when a demand reaches it, validated against its type's laws before it is applied
-- (10.S8 to 10.S10). What cannot be tested (the disk persists while the program is off, the clock
-- is monotone) is marked assumed, so the certificate of a run says under what hypotheses it holds
-- (10.S13, 6.S10).
--
-- Index. Status of each section: done, or todo with what it needs. A session or Codex continuing
-- this file takes the first todo, writes it in the pattern of the done ones, checks it with
-- `luthier parse src/luthier/lib/world.lth`, sets its status to done here, and commits.
--
--   §1 The token and actions: an action's type says it is one; `act`; `own`; state
--      actions idempotent, world actions never (10.S3 to 10.S6; D4, D8).               done
--   §2 Places and observations: the world as the library represents it; an action changes
--      only the places its declaration names (10.S7); what is assumed (10.S13).        done
--   §3 Externs as routes: read at the first demand with the hash of what was read in the
--      identity, validated against the type's laws, refused if they fail, provisional as
--      debt (10.S8 to 10.S10, L11.2, L11.7).                                           done
--   §4 The actions the first slice needs: `print`, reading a file (10.S2; D8).          done
--   §5 Named worlds: an extern with no law runs in one, priced as an assumption (10.S12,
--      L11.6). Waits for evidence (certification.lth).                                 todo
--   §6 The externs of sonolocalización (numpy and its data formats). Waits for the
--      inventory, record/IA/sonolocalizacion-inventory-2026-10-13/.                     todo

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- §1 The token and actions

-- An action is a function that takes the token first and gives the token after with its result,
-- `W * A` (10.S3, 10.S4; D8: a `main` with `W` returns `W * A`; 2.S17 writes `w1 <- print w0 "x"`):
-- its type says it is an action, and a function that mentions no `W` holds no effect (10.S1).
-- `act` applies it. The token has no `==` (4.R8); a program constructs none, it enters at
-- `main` or in the session (D8: `W` optional in `main`, a `main` without it a pure script).
Action (A : U) = W -> W * A
act (A : U) (a : Action A) (w : W) : W * A := a w
after (A : U) (p : W * A) : W := match p { (w, x) -> w }
result (A : U) (p : W * A) : A := match p { (w, x) -> x }

-- Two kinds of action (10.S6): one on Luthier's own store (writing a certificate, recording a
-- test) is idempotent and may be served from the memo; one on the world runs each time it is
-- demanded. `own` says which, from the action's declaration and not from its effect; an action
-- that declares nothing is a world action, the safer reading. The token is used exactly once
-- (D4): an action whose new token nobody uses is not demanded, so every action lies on the chain
-- that reaches the output; that is a rule of the checker over blocks (program.lth, 4.R12), not a
-- law here.
Declared (A : U) = { run : Action A, own : Bool, places : List String }

-- An observable world: the library says what a place is (a file path, the standard output, the
-- clock) and what is read at one; the token has no `==`, so two tokens are told apart only by
-- what is read at the places. The
-- laws a world owes: an own action done twice leaves what is read everywhere as once (10.S6); an
-- action changes only the places its declaration names (10.S7, §2).
World : {
  places : List String,
  read : W -> String -> Maybe String,
}
sameAt (m : World) (w v : W) (ps : List String) : Bool :=
  match ps { nil -> true ; cons p rest -> m.read w p == m.read v p and sameAt m w v rest }
-- an own action is idempotent: running it on its own result reads the same everywhere
OwnIsIdempotent (m : World) (A : U) (a : Declared A) (w : W) : Bool :=
  not a.own or sameAt m (after A (a.run w)) (after A (a.run (after A (a.run w)))) m.places

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- §2 Places and what an action changes

-- An action may change the world and Luthier's store, and nothing else (10.S7): not a definition,
-- not a binding of another block. In the world, it changes only the places its declaration names:
-- at every other place, what is read after is what was read before. That is the law of the world's
-- library over the observations it defines, certified as any law (D-2026-10-13-a).
except (ps xs : List String) : List String :=
  match ps { nil -> nil ; cons p rest -> if memberStr p xs then except rest xs else cons p (except rest xs) }
memberStr (x : String) (xs : List String) : Bool := match xs { nil -> false ; cons y rest -> x == y or memberStr x rest }
ChangesOnlyDeclared (m : World) (A : U) (a : Declared A) (w : W) : Bool :=
  sameAt m w (after A (a.run w)) (except m.places a.places)

-- What cannot be tested is assumed, so that the certificate of a run says under what hypotheses it
-- holds (10.S13, 6.S10): what is written at a place is read back at it (the disk persists); a
-- place no action touched reads the same after the program was off. Stated as laws, their
-- evidence is a citation or an assumption (laws.lth, `Assumed`), never a sample.
Persistence : {
  world : World,
  write : W -> String -> String -> W,
}
WriteThenRead (s : Persistence) (w : W) (p c : String) : Bool :=
  not (memberStr p s.world.places) or s.world.read (s.write w p c) p == just c
WriteChangesOnlyItsPlace (s : Persistence) (w : W) (p c : String) : Bool :=
  sameAt s.world w (s.write w p c) (except s.world.places [p])
Persistence := WriteThenRead & WriteChangesOnlyItsPlace

World := OwnIsIdempotent & ChangesOnlyDeclared

import "test.lth" TestSpec Tester
import "laws.lth" Evidence Verdict Refuted Confirmed Supported Unsettled Cites Assumes

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- §3 Externs as routes

-- An extern is a name, a declared type and a route to a host function: data (3.S40, 10.S8). Its
-- file is read when a demand reaches the node, once per plan, and may be absent while the program
-- is checked (L11.7); the hash of the bytes read, computed by the core, enters the identity of what
-- the extern produces and of its certificate (10.S9). `hash` is the core's (spec 6.S8), declared
-- here until core names it.
hash : String -> String
Route = { name : String, route : String, provisional : Bool }

-- The first time an action asks for an extern, before it is applied, it is tested against the
-- laws of its declared type like any function, and refused if it fails (10.S10, L11.2): the
-- validation is a test of test.lth, its verdict read from the evidence by laws.lth's `Settling`;
-- a refutation refuses, and an extern whose type has no law to validate against is not applied
-- (L11.3), unless it is provisional, which is applied, recorded as debt and never certified
-- (4.R5). The certificate stays by hash: a second demand with the same hash does not test again.
Externs : {
  read : W -> Route -> W * Maybe String,
  verdictOf : Route -> Verdict,
  applied : Route -> Bool,
  identity : Route -> String -> String,
}
-- refused when its type's laws are refuted; not applied when nothing settles them, unless provisional
RefusedWhenRefuted (e : Externs) (r : Route) : Bool :=
  not (e.verdictOf r == Refuted) or not (e.applied r)
NotAppliedWithoutLaw (e : Externs) (r : Route) : Bool :=
  r.provisional or not (e.verdictOf r == Unsettled) or not (e.applied r)
ProvisionalIsApplied (e : Externs) (r : Route) : Bool := not r.provisional or e.applied r
-- the identity of what it produces carries the hash of what was read, so two reads of different
-- bytes are two identities
IdentityCarriesHash (e : Externs) (r : Route) (bytes other : String) : Bool :=
  bytes == other or not (e.identity r (hash bytes) == e.identity r (hash other))
Externs := RefusedWhenRefuted & NotAppliedWithoutLaw & ProvisionalIsApplied & IdentityCarriesHash

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- §4 The actions the first slice needs

-- `print` appends its text to the standard output, a place of the world, and changes nothing
-- else; reading a file gives what is at its place, and changes nothing (10.S2, D8: `print` is the
-- first action). Both take the token first, as 2.S17 writes them. The model is world_model.lut,
-- whose operations are externs to the host (10.S8); the first program with an action is
-- examples/hello.lut.
Console : {
  world : World,
  print : W -> String -> W * Unit,
  readFile : W -> String -> W * Maybe String,
  stdout : String,
}
appendStr : String -> String -> String
PrintAppends (c : Console) (w : W) (s : String) : Bool :=
  c.world.read (after Unit (c.print w s)) c.stdout
    == (match c.world.read w c.stdout { nothing -> just s ; just prior -> just (appendStr prior s) })
PrintChangesOnlyStdout (c : Console) (w : W) (s : String) : Bool :=
  sameAt c.world w (after Unit (c.print w s)) (except c.world.places [c.stdout])
ReadGivesThePlace (c : Console) (w : W) (p : String) : Bool :=
  result (Maybe String) (c.readFile w p) == c.world.read w p
ReadChangesNothing (c : Console) (w : W) (p : String) : Bool :=
  sameAt c.world w (after (Maybe String) (c.readFile w p)) c.world.places
Console := PrintAppends & PrintChangesOnlyStdout & ReadGivesThePlace & ReadChangesNothing
